{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"GAP 5 — THE WIRING IS INCOMPLETE IN PRODUCTION. Found by the [operator-attested] criterion, which is\nexactly what that criterion exists for. This is the most important finding on this ticket.\n\nEVIDENCE: ran a real `rebar review-plan 7a79 --ref HEAD` with the current code live (the worktree venv is\nan editable install, so provenance_for IS in the running build). The freshly written REVIEW_RESULT\nsidecar contains:\n    model               : claude-sonnet-4-6\n    provider_provenance : ABSENT\nSo the field never reaches a production payload, even though all 10 unit tests pass.\n\nWHY THE TESTS MISSED IT: every test hands `build_payload` a verdict dict that ALREADY contains\nprovider_provenance. That validates the sidecar-write half of the contract and nothing else. The\nproduction question — does the verdict that reaches build_payload ever CARRY provenance — was never\nasserted. This is a textbook constructed-input pass: the oracle proved the persist step, not the path.\n\nROOT CAUSE: there are TWO different verdict objects and only one is wired.\n  - `runner.run()` -> `findings.finalize_outcome(..., provider_provenance=...)` (runner.py:372 builds it,\n    :611 threads it) produces a PER-CALL result that does carry it. That path is correct.\n  - The plan-review GATE verdict is assembled separately by\n    `plan_review/orchestrator.py::finalize_verdict` (the dict returned at :604-617, which sets\n    `\"model\": model` at :616). That function's signature is\n    `finalize_verdict(ctx, parts, *, coaching, coverage, runner_name, model)` — it receives NO cfg, so it\n    has neither `base_url` nor the provider name and cannot assemble provenance today. It simply never\n    sets the key, and `sidecar.build_payload`'s defensive `verdict.get(\"provider_provenance\")` then\n    writes None.\nThe same shape almost certainly applies to the code-review gate verdict; verify before assuming.\n\nFIX SHAPE (not yet done): thread provenance into `finalize_verdict` — either accept a\n`provider_provenance` argument from the callers that DO hold cfg (workflow_ops / gate_dispatch /\ndrift_refresh), or pass cfg itself. Prefer passing the already-assembled record rather than cfg, to keep\n`finalize_verdict` free of config knowledge and to preserve the \"assemble once, carry, never recompute\"\nrule this story established.\n\nTHE MISSING ORACLE: the new test must assert on the verdict produced by the REAL assembly path\n(finalize_verdict, or an end-to-end review-plan whose sidecar is then read), not on a hand-built verdict\ndict. Written as a RED test first, it should fail exactly as production does today.\n\nSTATUS OF THE OTHER FOUR GAPS: (1) persisted-payload credential test — DONE, mutation-verified (.netloc\nreintroduces the leak and the test catches it in the PAYLOAD, not just the record). (2) legacy-payload\ntest — DONE, mutation-verified (.get -> [] raises KeyError and the test catches it). (3)\ndocs/event-schema.md — DONE, new \"Provider provenance\" section documenting the field as additive and\noptional, alongside the existing session/creation-channel provenance sections. (4) the operator-attested\nlive evidence — ATTEMPTED, and it is what surfaced this gap; it cannot be satisfied until the wiring\nlands. Do NOT close this story until gap 5 is fixed and the live evidence actually shows a populated\nprovider_provenance."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785462667492229001,"uuid":"56ea5808-b558-49f3-914e-2dc6887e6992"}